```apex
public with sharing class FollowUpTaskService {
    /**
     * Creates a follow-up Task for each Opportunity.
     *
     * Not implemented as per-record insert in a try/catch: a weekly review
     * passes 150–250 Opportunities in one call, which would exceed the 150
     * DML-statement governor limit. Database.insert(..., false) is one DML
     * statement, stays bulk-safe, and still lets validation-rule failures on
     * individual Tasks leave the rest created.
     *
     * @param opportunityIds Opportunity Ids from the weekly pipeline review
     * @return Ids of Opportunities whose Task could not be created
     */
    public static Set<Id> createFollowUps(List<Id> opportunityIds) {
        Set<Id> failedOpportunityIds = new Set<Id>();
        if (opportunityIds == null || opportunityIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        Set<Id> uniqueIds = new Set<Id>();
        for (Id opportunityId : opportunityIds) {
            if (opportunityId != null) {
                uniqueIds.add(opportunityId);
            }
        }
        if (uniqueIds.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Opportunity> opportunities = [
            SELECT Id, Name, OwnerId
            FROM Opportunity
            WHERE Id IN :uniqueIds
        ];

        Set<Id> foundIds = new Set<Id>();
        List<Task> tasks = new List<Task>();
        for (Opportunity opportunity : opportunities) {
            foundIds.add(opportunity.Id);
            tasks.add(
                new Task(
                    WhatId = opportunity.Id,
                    OwnerId = opportunity.OwnerId,
                    Subject = 'Follow up: ' + opportunity.Name,
                    ActivityDate = Date.today().addDays(7),
                    Status = 'Not Started',
                    Priority = 'Normal'
                )
            );
        }

        // No Task can be created for Ids that were not queryable.
        failedOpportunityIds.addAll(uniqueIds);
        failedOpportunityIds.removeAll(foundIds);

        if (tasks.isEmpty()) {
            return failedOpportunityIds;
        }

        List<Database.SaveResult> results = Database.insert(tasks, false);
        for (Integer i = 0; i < results.size(); i++) {
            if (!results[i].isSuccess()) {
                failedOpportunityIds.add(tasks[i].WhatId);
            }
        }

        return failedOpportunityIds;
    }
}
```

I did a few things differently from the “one `@future` per opportunity” sketch, because that design would fail in production:

- **One future call per transaction, not per opportunity.** A synchronous transaction may enqueue at most 50 `@future` jobs. CPQ batches of 200 would blow that limit and roll back the save. The trigger collects **unique** `AccountId`s and passes them to a single bulk `@future` method.
- **`without sharing`.** Rating must reflect the whole open pipeline, not only opportunities the running user can see.
- **Also recalc when `AccountId` changes** (old and new account). Otherwise a reparented opportunity leaves both accounts stale. Insert + `StageName`/`Amount` changes are still the primary entry points.
- **Skip no-op Account updates** and use `MAX(Amount)` so the async job stays cheap.

Delete/undelete is left out of the trigger because it was not requested; add `after delete`/`after undelete` if you want Rating to stay correct when pipeline rows disappear.

